#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# coulomb_f

#% text
# `coulomb_f(l, eta, rho)` gives the regular Coulomb wave function `F_l(eta, rho)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `F_l(eta, rho) = C_l(eta) rho^(l + 1) e^(-i rho) M(l + 1 - i eta, 2 l + 2, 2 i rho)` with `C_l(eta) = 2^l e^(-pi eta/2) |gamma(l + 1 + i eta)|/gamma(2 l + 2)` (DLMF 33.2.4-33.2.5); `|gamma|` continues to complex `l` and `eta` as `exp((log_gamma(l + 1 + i eta) + log_gamma(l + 1 - i eta))/2)`.

#% item
# At `eta = 0`, `F_l(0, rho) = rho j_l(rho)`.

#% item
# Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
coulomb_f(3, 0.12, 7.0)
#% output
#= 0.15899181007331104

#% cell
n(coulomb_f(3, 2, 5), 30)
#% output
#= 0.43131593114087869012387960004177853

#% cell
coulomb_f(2/3, 0.7 + 0.1*im, 3.2)
#% output
#= 1.132683472485845 - 0.01993514125618819im

#% cell
coulomb_f(l, 0, r)
#% output
#= r*spherical_bessel_j(l, r)

#% text
# A strongly repulsive field keeps the regular solution tiny near the origin; its digits are kept:

#% cell
n(coulomb_f(1/2, 7, 1), 20)
#% output
#= 9.26469883853782447175694e-8

